  {"id":69182,"slug":"ostrea-lurida","sci":"Ostrea lurida","common":"Olympia Oyster","family":"Ostreidae","obs":108,"regions":[],"months":[2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9,10],"photos":[{"id":514899505,"ext":"jpg","by":"Evan Hessels","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":402673896,"ext":"jpg","by":"Rick Harbo","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":113396778,"ext":"jpeg","by":"an iNaturalist observer","lic":"CC0"},{"id":212931703,"ext":"jpeg","by":"humon","lic":"CC BY"},{"id":5206881,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Alison Young","lic":"CC BY-NC"}],"wiki":"Ostrea lurida","summary":"Ostrea lurida, common name the Olympia oyster, after Olympia, Washington in the Puget Sound area, is a species of edible oyster, a marine bivalve mollusk in the family Ostreidae. This species occurs on the northern Pacific coast of North America. Over the years the role of this edible species of oyster has been partly displaced by the cultivation of non-native edible oyster species."},
